A complete route has emerged. Writing \(r(x)=p(x)-x\), the second-order polynomial Taylor identity gives
\[
r(x+r(x))=r(x)+r'(x)r(x)+r(x)^2s(x).
\]
Thus the required divisibility forces \(r\mid(2+r')\). If \(r\) is nonconstant, its degree is larger than that of \(2+r'\), so \(2+r'=0\), forcing \(p(x)=-x+c\); constant \(r\) gives \(p(x)=x+c\). I’m now recording this proof and submitting the exact original question as the target fact.
