Verified the complete solution as fact `68e29d0676764226`.

The solutions are exactly
\[
p(x)=x+c \quad\text{or}\quad p(x)=-x+c,\qquad c\in\mathbb R.
\]

Set \(r=p-x\). Expanding \(r(x+r(x))\) modulo \(r(x)^2\) yields
\[
p(p(x))-x=r(x)(2+r'(x))+r(x)^2s(x).
\]
Hence the required identity forces \(r\mid 2+r'\). If \(r\) is nonconstant, degree comparison implies \(r'=-2\), giving \(p=-x+c\); constant \(r\) gives \(p=x+c\). Direct substitution verifies both families. The verifier found no gaps or critical errors.
